Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 23:44:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 110/154] feat(sidebar): frost the page behind the floating
 panel
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The panel was translucent with nothing behind it, and this task had been
closed once on the finding that a real blur was unreachable. That finding
was wrong. It rested on the note that the web content's NSView composites
above its views siblings, which governs Views compositor paint, not
NSViews - a manually added NSView does paint over the page, as the overlay
shadow layer now demonstrates.

AtriumSidebarFrostLayer wraps an NSVisualEffectView in a plain NSView and
tracks the panel's frame. The wrapper is load-bearing: any bare
NSVisualEffectView is sorted to the rearmost band on the next resort,
which a tab switch triggers, and the comparator only inspects direct
subviews.

Proven by a differential rather than a threshold. Saturation alone cannot
separate a blurred page from the material's own tint, so the test is
whether the sampled colour MOVES with the page behind it. It does, by 179
where two pages differ by 212.

The wash floor drops from .96 to .40 because its job changed. It existed
to hide page text, and the frost now does that at any floor. The wash is
the greater of the user's Glass opacity and this floor, so .96 was
overriding a live .55 setting and hiding the frost behind it. The comments
that called the floor the legibility guarantee are rewritten rather than
left to mislead the next reader.

The frost installs below the shadow layer by name rather than relying on
where an undocumented sort leaves two unassociated views. A shadow cast by
a floating surface should sit on the frost, not be smeared by it.

--atrium-disable-sidebar-frost is a kill switch, not a test hook: the
frost has no user-facing setting of its own to fall back on.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
